<title>YUGOSLAVIA: Yugoslav May oil imports soar, gas imports slump.</title>
<headline>Yugoslav May oil imports soar, gas imports slump.</headline>
<dateline>BELGRADE 1997-06-05</dateline>
<text>
<p>The Federal Statistics Office reported the following economic indicator details.</p>
<p> CRUDE OIL IMPORTS		    MAY     APRIL    MAY 96</p>
<p> In tonnes			  209,778    69,762    22,684</p>
<p> In million USD			  29	  11	   4</p>
<p>OIL DERIVATIVES IMPORTS</p>
<p> In tonnes			   59,811     4,763    83,981</p>
<p> In million USD			  15	   3	  17</p>
<p>NATURAL GAS IMPORTS</p>
<p> In tonnes			   13,558   150,968	 397</p>
<p> In million USD			   2	  21	  --</p>
<p>NOTES -- Cumulative January-May crude oil imports stood at 974,737 tonnes and were worth a total of $165 million.</p>
<p>Cumulative oil derivatives imports in the first five months of 1997 amounted to 232,353 tonnes and totalled $62 million.</p>
<p>Cumulative natural gas imports for the same period stood at 305,052 tonnes and were worth $43 million.</p>
<p>Figures refer to Federal Republic of Yugoslavia, comprising Serbia and Montegro.</p>
<p>-- Amra Kevic, Belgrade newsroom +381 11 222 4305</p>
